Games and Artificial Intelligence
Course: Artificial Intelligence in Games
Project type: Practical, project-based learning
Prerequisites: Proficiency in Object-Oriented Programming (C++, C#, or Python) and Data Structures & Algorithms.

1. Project overview
The following eight projects are designed to help students understand, implement, and evaluate different AI techniques used in modern video games.

The projects progress from classical game AI techniques to more advanced approaches involving machine learning, reinforcement learning, and generative AI.

They are designed to be achievable within a three-week course, with a working prototype and a final demonstration as the expected outcomes.

Students can work in small teams of 3-4 members.

Project 3. Tactical Enemy AI for Combat Games
Intermediate

Decision-making AI


Project description

In many action and shooter games, enemies should behave intelligently rather than simply running toward the player and attacking.

Students will design an enemy AI system capable of making tactical decisions based on the player's position, health, available cover, distance, and the surrounding environment.

For example, an enemy may decide to attack when the player is vulnerable, retreat when its health is low, move behind cover when under fire, or attempt to flank the player when another enemy is distracting them.

The objective is to develop an AI decision-making system that produces believable and challenging combat encounters.

Technical implementation

1. Game environment

·       Create a small combat arena with walls, obstacles, and cover positions.

·       Define the player's location, health, and combat status.

·       Create several enemy agents with different attributes.

2. Decision-making system

·       Implement Utility AI to assign scores to possible actions.

·       Define possible actions such as:

o   Attack the player.

o   Take cover.

o   Retreat.

o   Reload.

o   Flank the player.

o   Support another enemy.

·       Calculate action scores based on contextual information.

3. Tactical navigation

·       Use A* pathfinding to reach cover positions.

·       Calculate suitable flanking routes.

·       Avoid obstacles and dangerous areas.

·       Select positions based on distance and tactical advantage.

4. Multi-agent coordination

·       Allow enemies to share information about the player's location.

·       Assign different combat roles.

·       Avoid enemies selecting identical positions.

·       Introduce simple coordination between agents.

5. Evaluation

·       Compare enemy behavior with and without tactical decision-making.

·       Evaluate decision quality, response time, and combat effectiveness.

Environment Format: Students may implement the combat environment in either 2D (top-down perspective) or 3D (third-person perspective). The choice of environment does not affect the grading criteria. Evaluation will focus primarily on the correctness, intelligence, and performance of the tactical AI system.

Expected deliverables

·       A playable combat arena.

·       At least three enemy agents.

·       A Utility AI decision-making system.

·       Tactical behaviors such as taking cover, retreating, and flanking.

·       A demonstration showing different combat situations.

Suggested tools: Unity with C#, Unreal Engine with C++, or Python.

Possible extension: Introduce a learning-based system that adjusts enemy tactical decisions based on player behavior.